<ArticleLayout title="Compare child savings account types" description="A purpose-first comparison of Trump Accounts, 529 plans, UGMA/UTMA custodial accounts, Custodial Roth IRAs, and ABLE accounts." sourceIds={['TREASURY-TD-10056','IRS-FORM-4547-INSTRUCTIONS','IRS-529-QA']} effective="Federal rules reviewed through October 8, 2026">
  <p>No single account is “best” for every child. The useful question is what the money is for, who should control it, and which tax rules fit that goal.</p>
  <div style="overflow-x:auto">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Account</th><th scope="col">Primary purpose</th><th scope="col">Control</th><th scope="col">Key constraint</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Trump Account</strong></td><td>Long-term child investing with special rules before 18</td><td>Responsible party before adulthood; child owns the account</td><td>Restricted investments and withdrawals during growth period; later generally follows traditional IRA rules</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>529 plan</strong></td><td>Education savings</td><td>Account owner generally retains control</td><td>Federal tax advantages depend on qualified use; state rules and incentives vary</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>UGMA/UTMA</strong></td><td>Flexible irrevocable gift to a minor</td><td>Custodian until state transfer age, then the child</td><td>Gift belongs to the child; state transfer-age, tax, and financial-aid treatment require separate review</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Custodial Roth IRA</strong></td><td>Retirement saving for a child with earned income</td><td>Custodian while minor; child later</td><td>Child needs eligible compensation; annual IRA limits apply</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>ABLE account</strong></td><td>Qualified disability expenses and long-term support</td><td>Eligible individual or authorized person</td><td>Eligibility and qualified-expense rules apply</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h2>Start with the goal</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Education is the primary goal:</strong> begin by studying 529 rules and your state’s plan incentives.</li>
    <li><strong>The child may receive the $1,000 pilot contribution:</strong> check auto-account and pilot-election status even if another account remains the main savings vehicle.</li>
    <li><strong>Maximum spending flexibility matters:</strong> learn the irrevocable ownership and state transfer-age rules of UGMA/UTMA accounts, then verify current financial-aid treatment from official sources.</li>
    <li><strong>The child has earned income:</strong> a Custodial Roth IRA may create a distinct retirement pathway.</li>
    <li><strong>Disability-related eligibility applies:</strong> ABLE rules deserve a dedicated comparison before using a general custodial account.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout">This comparison is educational, not a recommendation. State tax treatment, financial-aid treatment, benefits eligibility, and family circumstances can change the result.</div>
